menuconfig SECURITY_HARDENING
	bool "Hardening contract layer"
	depends on SECURITY
	depends on ARCH_HAS_SET_MEMORY
	depends on MMU
	select SECURITYFS
	select MODULE_SIG_FORMAT
	select MODULE_SIG if MODULES
	select MODULE_SIG_FORCE if MODULES
	help
	  Provide a small kernel hardening contract layer for distro policy.
	  The layer centralizes authority checks and dangerous transition
	  mediation while leaving product policy, prompts, and profile
	  compilation to userspace.

	  This is not an LSM policy language. It is a kernel-side enforcement
	  surface for sealed profiles, authority gates, and structured audit.
	  Policy blobs must carry an appended PKCS#7 signature trusted by the
	  kernel keyrings before they can be loaded, and the architecture must
	  support kernel page-permission changes so published policy tables can
	  be sealed read-only.

if SECURITY_HARDENING

config HDN_ARCH_CONTRACT
	def_bool X86_64 || ARM64 || RISCV

config SECURITY_HARDENING_ARCH_CONTRACT_TEST
	bool "Architecture capability contract unit fixtures"
	depends on HDN_ARCH_CONTRACT && KUNIT=y
	default n
	help
	  Enumerate every capability state and backing value, validate the complete
	  architecture descriptor inventory, and check which states may satisfy a
	  signed policy requirement. Enable only in qualification kernels.

config SECURITY_HARDENING_SYSFS_VIEW
	bool "Signed profile sysfs discovery views"
	depends on SYSFS
	default n
	help
	  Honor signed profile restrictions on kernel, firmware, device and
	  module discovery. Class membership follows registered kernfs nodes,
	  their ancestors and symlink targets. Check the current application,
	  mount namespace and user namespace on each operation, including reads
	  through transferred descriptors. This reduces discovery; it does not
	  revoke information already read or copied through an existing mapping.
	  Profiles without visibility restrictions retain the base system view.

config SECURITY_HARDENING_KERNEL_WX
	bool "Reject x86 kernel W+X permission transitions"
	depends on X86_64 && STRICT_KERNEL_RWX
	select IRQ_WORK
	default n
	help
	  Reject page-attribute transitions that would make a present kernel
	  mapping both writable and executable. Callers must remove execute
	  permission before making code writable, then remove write permission
	  before enabling execution. CPA and vmalloc also reject writable aliases
	  of finalized built-in text and read-only data by physical page number.
	  Temporary text-poking aliases carry task/CPU-bound transition tokens;
	  their PFNs and permissions are checked before use and their removal is
	  checked after TLB synchronization. Debugger/NMI events use a bounded
	  deferred mailbox without acquiring audit locks in stopped-CPU contexts;
	  mailbox overflows are counted explicitly.
	  Dynamic-image aliases, descriptors, and page-table lifecycle coverage
	  are separate contracts and must not be inferred from this switch alone.

config SECURITY_HARDENING_GDT
	bool "Protect GDT and IDT backing aliases"
	depends on SECURITY_HARDENING_KERNEL_WX
	default n
	help
	  Remove writable backing aliases of native per-CPU GDTs. Descriptor
	  writes and the hardware LTR busy-bit update use preallocated per-CPU
	  supervisor-only, non-global, NX windows. Separate emergency contexts
	  cover terminal NMI/crash paths; raw CR3 preservation covers AP setup
	  before software TLB state exists. Xen PV keeps its hypervisor protocol.
	  Finalized IDT pages reject writable aliases and data text-poking.
	  This is a qualification option, not a measured release default.

config SECURITY_HARDENING_LDT
	bool "Seal replacement local descriptor tables"
	depends on SECURITY_HARDENING_GDT && SECURITY_HARDENING_EXECMEM
	depends on MODIFY_LDT_SYSCALL
	default n
	help
	  Allocate each LDT in private page-backed storage with its direct-map
	  alias removed. Seal before publication, retain only the canonical
	  read-only view and the fixed read-only PTI slot, and replace whole
	  tables on modification. Teardown waits for the existing LDT IPI and
	  mapping lifetime, then scrubs and frees in process context. Xen uses
	  the same sealed storage and its normal hypervisor descriptor loading.

config SECURITY_HARDENING_INITIFY
	bool "Retire kernel image aliases before reclaiming init storage"
	depends on SECURITY_HARDENING_KERNEL_WX
	default n
	help
	  Remove the high kernel image mapping of reclaimed initialization pages
	  before returning those pages to the allocator, including when PTI is
	  inactive. Poison and reclaim through the direct map. Stale init symbol
	  references then fault instead of reaching a reused allocation. Permission
	  transition failures stop boot before reclamation. Static lifetime proof
	  still requires the compiler, relocation and modpost verification gate.

config SECURITY_HARDENING_EXECMEM
	bool "Private physical pages for executable allocations"
	depends on SECURITY_HARDENING_KERNEL_WX && EXECMEM && ARCH_HAS_EXECMEM_ROX
	depends on STRICT_MODULE_RWX
	default n
	help
	  Give fresh executable allocations a single construction mapping, remove
	  their direct-map aliases before publication, and reject new aliases by
	  physical page number. Finalization is irreversible; live updates use
	  the scoped text-poking window. Teardown revokes execution and scrubs
	  before unmapping, flushing, and returning pages to the allocator.
	  Whole allocations replace the shared execmem ROX cache. Enable in a
	  qualification profile before considering release promotion.

config SECURITY_HARDENING_PGTABLE
	bool "Seal native x86 page-table backing storage"
	depends on SECURITY_HARDENING_EXECMEM && X86_64
	depends on !KMSAN
	default n
	help
	  Own and seal ordinary page tables from boot through TLB/RCU retirement.
	  Typed native setters use guarded per-CPU private CR3 update windows;
	  atomic allocation and CPA splitting use presealed NUMA-aware reserves.
	  Initial direct, image, PTI, EFI, and trampoline aliases are reconciled.
	  Kexec and hibernation contexts are immutable before publication or
	  quiescence; they belong to the terminal handoff, not ordinary CR3s.
	  Xen PV and confidential-memory guests report unsupported rather than
	  claiming that native windows replace their hypervisor protocols.
	  Keep disabled in release profiles until the transition, sanitizer,
	  source-write-site, and performance qualification gates have passed.

config SECURITY_HARDENING_PGTABLE_TEST
	bool "Page-table ownership and destructive write probes"
	depends on SECURITY_HARDENING_PGTABLE && DEBUG_KERNEL && MODULES
	default n
	help
	  Add a post-boot signed test-module entrypoint. Positive mode exercises
	  the real allocation, native setter, atomic update, alias, and release
	  interfaces. Destructive modes require a fresh disposable VM each.
	  Do not enable this entrypoint in production configurations.

config SECURITY_HARDENING_RAP
	bool "Native x86-64 return and indirect-transfer integrity with private stacks"
	depends on X86_64 && SECURITY_HARDENING_PGTABLE && VMAP_STACK
	depends on OBJTOOL && !COMPILE_TEST
	depends on CC_IS_GCC && GCC_PLUGINS
	depends on GCC_VERSION >= 160000 && GCC_VERSION < 170000
	depends on !RUST && !CFI_CLANG && !LTO
	depends on !XEN_PV && !AMD_MEM_ENCRYPT && !INTEL_TDX_GUEST
	depends on !HYPERV_VTL_MODE
	select OBJTOOL_WERROR
	select SECURITY_HARDENING_GDT
	select SECURITY_HARDENING_LDT if MODIFY_LDT_SYSCALL
	select SECURITY_HARDENING_INITIFY
	select SECURITY_HARDENING_BPF_EXECMEM if BPF_JIT
	default n
	help
	  Compile C and assembly transfers with matching type, return-site and
	  protected return-context contracts. Bind task and asynchronous frames
	  to private stack mappings and sealed CPU identities. Module admission
	  requires the same compiler, build identity and metadata ABI. Objtool
	  rejects uncovered transfers; the real-mode image has a separate gate.

	  The initial compiler ABI is GCC 16. Clang, Rust, Xen PV, confidential
	  guests and Hyper-V VTL kernels do not have a supported implementation
	  of this combined contract. They cannot silently omit instrumentation.
	  Native IA32/x32 compatibility, tracing, BPF, KVM, suspend and crash
	  paths remain subject to the same transfer and ownership checks.

	  Select the source/config/compiler manifest digest below before building.
	  This is a default-off qualification option. Build coverage is required
	  but does not establish runtime correctness or acceptable overhead.

config SECURITY_HARDENING_PRIVATE_STACKS
	def_bool SECURITY_HARDENING_RAP
	help
	  The initial private-stack and RAP implementations are one contract:
	  neither may be selected without the other's entry and return adapters.
	  Task backing loses its direct and inactive aliases. Per-CPU roots expose
	  the current task and dedicated interrupt/exception/failure stacks only.
	  Privileged readers copy bounded data through owned temporary mappings.

config HDN_RAP_BUILD_ID
	string "RAP source/config/compiler manifest SHA-256"
	depends on SECURITY_HARDENING_RAP
	default ""
	help
	  Enter the 64 lowercase hexadecimal digits produced by the HDN RAP
	  identity command for the final source, normalized configuration and
	  compiler. The digest excludes this field to avoid self-reference.
	  An empty or malformed identity is a compiler error. Reusing an old
	  identity after changing its inputs fails the source-bound build gate.
	  This is public build identity, not a secret or a runtime policy knob.

config SECURITY_HARDENING_RAP_ERROR_TEST
	bool "RAP function error-injection lifecycle probes"
	depends on SECURITY_HARDENING_RAP && FUNCTION_ERROR_INJECTION
	depends on KRETPROBES && DEBUG_KERNEL && MODULES
	default n
	help
	  Build a signed module loader for post-boot entry-probe, repeated error
	  override, return-hook and unregister/reuse checks. Destructive modes
	  try a changed stack pointer or an unmarked function and require a
	  separate disposable guest. No error-injection API is newly exported.

config SECURITY_HARDENING_RAP_TEST
	bool "RAP transfer and return-integrity probes (disposable guests only)"
	depends on SECURITY_HARDENING_RAP && DEBUG_KERNEL && MODULES
	depends on FRAME_POINTER && HIGH_RES_TIMERS && X86_LOCAL_APIC
	default n
	help
	  Build a signed module containing positive typed callbacks, task-switch,
	  timer-IRQ and NMI paths plus destructive type-confusion, overwritten-return
	  and copied-return cases. Each destructive mode must run in a fresh guest.
	  This interface exposes no production control and must remain disabled in
	  release configurations.

config SECURITY_HARDENING_KVM_STATE_TEST
	bool "Virtualization host-state ownership probes"
	depends on SECURITY_HARDENING_PRIVATE_STACKS && DEBUG_KERNEL && MODULES
	depends on KVM_INTEL || KVM_AMD
	default n
	help
	  Build post-boot signed-module probes for real hardware-state allocation,
	  alias rejection, and protected VMCS/host-save writes. Destructive modes
	  require a separate disposable test guest. This does not replace KVM
	  guest-entry, nested virtualization, or CPU hotplug qualification.

config SECURITY_HARDENING_PRIVATE_STACKS_TEST
	bool "Private kernel stack mapping probes"
	depends on SECURITY_HARDENING_PRIVATE_STACKS && DEBUG_KERNEL && MODULES
	default n
	help
	  Build a signed-module interface for private-stack lifecycle and alias
	  probes in disposable guests. Mode zero checks current and inactive
	  task ownership, guard pages, zero initialization, bounded diagnostic
	  reads, and withdrawn direct/construction aliases. Destructive modes
	  fault on lower/upper guards, withdrawn aliases, NX stack execution,
	  or a non-current task identity. This interface must remain disabled
	  in release configurations.

config SECURITY_HARDENING_KERNSEAL
	bool "Typed immutable policy and mitigation objects"
	depends on SECURITY_HARDENING_EXECMEM
	default n
	help
	  Allocate policy and mitigation payloads on private page granules.
	  Validate and seal once, publish with release ordering, and replace
	  entire objects. Retired objects stay read-only until RCU readers and
	  explicit references finish; reclamation then runs in process context.
	  No arbitrary module registration or runtime unseal operation exists.
	  Keep disabled in release profiles until class-specific qualification.

config SECURITY_HARDENING_BPF_EXECMEM
	bool "Isolate BPF executable allocations"
	depends on SECURITY_HARDENING_EXECMEM && BPF_JIT
	default n
	help
	  Reserve a randomized x86 branch-reachable executable domain for BPF
	  programs, trampolines and dispatchers. Exclude every other execmem
	  class, randomize allocation search starts, and retain vmalloc guard
	  pages. Capacity exhaustion fails the allocation without borrowing
	  module space. Physical ownership and one-way W^X use KERNEXEC.
	  Enable only in qualification profiles until JIT lifecycle and capacity
	  measurements have passed.

config SECURITY_HARDENING_BPF_EXECMEM_TEST
	bool "BPF executable-memory destructive probes"
	depends on SECURITY_HARDENING_BPF_EXECMEM && X86_64 && DEBUG_KERNEL && MODULES
	default n
	help
	  Add a built-in test service and signed module loader for fresh disposable
	  guests. Positive mode executes a finalized private image and rejects a
	  writable alias. Destructive modes execute during construction, write after
	  finalization, or execute a retired quarantined address and must terminate
	  the guest. Never enable this interface in production.

config SECURITY_HARDENING_KERNSEAL_TEST
	bool "KERNSEAL lifecycle and alias probes"
	depends on SECURITY_HARDENING_KERNSEAL && KUNIT=y && DEBUG_KERNEL && MODULES
	default n
	help
	  Add a test-only payload class and post-boot KUnit loader for lifecycle,
	  type, physical-alias, and permission failures. Do not ship this class
	  or loader in production configurations.

config SECURITY_HARDENING_CONSTIFY_TEST
	bool "Static and module post-init immutable-storage probes"
	depends on SECURITY_HARDENING_PGTABLE && KUNIT=y && DEBUG_KERNEL && MODULES
	default n
	help
	  Build a test-only target module containing post-init immutable data and
	  a second KUnit module that must be loaded after the target has completed
	  initialization. The split permits suspend, CPU-hotplug, and kexec-load
	  preparation between target and probe loads. Never enable these address
	  exposure and negative-write fixtures in a production configuration.

config SECURITY_HARDENING_KERNEL_WX_TEST
	tristate "KERNEXEC CPA and built-in alias test probes"
	depends on SECURITY_HARDENING_KERNEL_WX && KUNIT=y && DEBUG_KERNEL
	default n
	help
	  Build negative permission-transition and built-in physical-alias probes.
	  These deliberately attempt denied mappings without executing or writing
	  through them. Load as a module after boot to test finalized static
	  sections; built-in KUnit runs before read-only finalization and skips
	  that case. Use only in the qualification kernel, not production.

config SECURITY_HARDENING_LATENT_ENTROPY
	bool "Supplemental zero-credit execution-state mixing"
	depends on GCC_PLUGINS && !GCC_PLUGIN_LATENT_ENTROPY
	default n
	help
	  Instrument only the reviewed boot, fork and interrupt collection sites.
	  Mix defined control-flow state and legal timing samples into the random
	  pool without crediting entropy or advancing CRNG readiness. Collection
	  is sampled and uses per-CPU state; it never reads uninitialized storage.
	  Leave this off unless a measured entropy-starved platform benefits.
	  This is not a replacement for device, hardware or OS entropy sources.

config SECURITY_HARDENING_LATENT_ENTROPY_TEST
	bool "Deterministic latent-mixing test interface"
	depends on SECURITY_HARDENING_LATENT_ENTROPY && KUNIT=y && DEBUG_KERNEL
	default n
	help
	  Build a deterministic, CPU-local instrumentation fixture and KUnit
	  suite. The seed interface exists only in this test build, is not
	  exported, and cannot be enabled by signed policy or a runtime knob.
	  Never enable this option in a production release configuration.

config GCC_PLUGIN_HDN_SIZE_ANALYSIS
	bool "Report arithmetic feeding allocation and copy sizes"
	depends on GCC_PLUGINS
	default y
	help
	  Emit a source-linked inventory of arithmetic feeding allocation and
	  copy boundaries before inlining removes checked helper contracts.
	  Reports include unresolved dataflow and reviewed wrapping annotations;
	  exceptions are enforced by the external verification ledger. This
	  reporting pass does not change generated kernel code. Source-level
	  checked arithmetic remains enforced independently of this option.

config GCC_PLUGIN_HDN_LIFETIME
	bool "Record static object and initialization lifetimes"
	depends on GCC_PLUGINS
	default n
	help
	  Emit definitions, direct reads and writes, address escapes, call edges,
	  indirect stores and assembly sites before optimization. The external
	  CONSTIFY/INITIFY gate combines these records with ELF relocations and
	  a source-bound review ledger. Unresolved aliases and callbacks require
	  review; a missing edge never establishes a safe lifetime. This pass
	  reports evidence without changing generated code.

config GCC_PLUGIN_HDN_LIFETIME_FIXTURE
	bool "Build static lifetime positive fixtures"
	depends on GCC_PLUGIN_HDN_LIFETIME && DEBUG_KERNEL
	default n
	help
	  Compile the init-only, post-init immutable and cross-translation-unit
	  positive seeds into a qualification kernel. This option exists only to
	  exercise the source, ELF, modpost and objtool evidence pipeline.

config GCC_PLUGIN_HDN_LIFETIME_BAD_FIXTURE
	bool "Build intentionally invalid static lifetime fixtures"
	depends on GCC_PLUGIN_HDN_LIFETIME_FIXTURE
	default n
	help
	  Add mutable dispatch, post-seal write, runtime-to-init call, retained
	  init callback and cross-unit runtime-to-init references. A gate profile
	  selecting this option must be rejected; it is not a bootable test image.

config GCC_PLUGIN_HDN_PGTABLE_ANALYSIS
	bool "Record page-table mutation sites"
	depends on GCC_PLUGINS && X86_64
	default n
	help
	  Record typed page-table stores, bulk writes, mutation API calls,
	  unresolved indirect stores and assembly in page-table contexts before
	  optimization. The external KERNEXEC gate binds the complete compilation
	  inventory and final linked objects to an exact source-review ledger.
	  This evidence producer does not change generated kernel code and is not
	  a substitute for runtime page-table ownership enforcement.

config SECURITY_HARDENING_RESPECTRE
	bool "GCC transient dataflow instrumentation"
	depends on X86_64 && GCC_PLUGINS && CC_IS_GCC
	depends on GCC_VERSION >= 160000 && GCC_VERSION < 170000
	depends on !COMPILE_TEST
	depends on !AMD_MEM_ENCRYPT
	default n
	help
	  Track untrusted parameters, mutable memory and unknown call results.
	  Mask proven simple array bounds and serialize other selected loads,
	  stores and call boundaries. Software store-bypass coverage does not
	  depend on the runtime SSBD setting. Strict compiler fixtures reject an
	  unknown assembly boundary until a source-bound annotation describes it.
	  The release coverage gate also binds excluded assembly and compiler
	  units, review spans, compiler identity and final machine code. Kernel
	  compilation records unknown inline assembly as a mandatory review
	  obligation; the release gate rejects any obligation without an exact,
	  source-bound disposition. Standalone fixture compilation remains strict.
	  Uses CPUID serialization on x86-64; encrypted guests whose CPUID can
	  fault are unsupported. Clang has no equivalent producer and cannot
	  select this option. GCC versions outside the release GCC 16 producer ABI
	  cannot select it either. Leave disabled until compatibility, leakage and
	  workload cost have been qualified on representative hardware.

config SECURITY_HARDENING_RESPECTRE_TEST
	bool "RESPECTRE module semantic fixture"
	depends on SECURITY_HARDENING_RESPECTRE && MODULES && DEBUG_KERNEL
	default n
	help
	  Build a signed, post-boot module whose bounded index, pointer selection,
	  indirect-call and store/load paths must retain ordinary architectural
	  semantics while being compiled by the same RESPECTRE producer as other
	  modules. The fixture emits only a bounded pass/fail marker. It is for a
	  disposable qualification guest and must not be selected in production.

config SECURITY_HARDENING_UDEREF
	bool "Require audited x86 user-access windows"
	depends on SECURITY_HARDENING_KERNEL_WX && HAVE_UACCESS_VALIDATION
	depends on HAVE_OBJTOOL && !COMPILE_TEST && PRINTK
	select OBJTOOL
	select OBJTOOL_WERROR
	select STACKTRACE_BUILD_ID
	default n
	help
	  Use the existing SMAP access windows and pinned CR4 protection. Make
	  objtool's unbalanced, nested, callback and return-with-access-enabled
	  diagnostics fatal, and record attempts to change pinned SMAP state in
	  the deferred hardening event stream after the bit has been restored.
	  CPUs without SMAP report unsupported; this option does not claim a
	  software substitute or introduce a policy-controlled relaxation.

config SECURITY_HARDENING_UDEREF_TEST
	bool "HDN destructive user-access probes (disposable guests only)"
	depends on SECURITY_HARDENING_UDEREF && X86_64 && X86_LOCAL_APIC
	depends on DEBUG_KERNEL && MODULES
	default n
	help
	  Build a signed-module loader for real user-mapping access, fault fixup,
	  SMAP pinning and IRQ/NMI window probes. Direct read/write modes
	  deliberately crash the guest. This is not a production interface.

config SECURITY_HARDENING_AUDIT
	bool "Emit hardening denial diagnostics"
	default y
	help
	  Emit ratelimited kernel diagnostics when the hardening contract layer
	  denies an operation. A later userspace event channel should replace
	  this for product builds.
	  The message includes a stable authority, transition, and reason code.
	  It is intended for early bring-up and harness validation.

config SECURITY_HARDENING_AUDIT_FLOOD
	bool "Throttle repeated hardening audit events"
	default y
	help
	  Suppress repeated identical hardening audit events after a bounded
	  runtime-configurable burst/window. The first events remain visible
	  through securityfs and kernel diagnostics, while repeated floods are
	  counted in the hardening status file instead of continuously
	  evicting useful records from the event ring.

config SECURITY_HARDENING_EXEC_AUDIT
	bool "Record successful exec events"
	default y
	help
	  Record successful execve and execveat transitions into the hardening
	  event ring after credentials and the new profile are committed. The
	  EXEC event stores the executable inode as the event object, and the
	  EXEC_ARGS event stores argv and environment counts as
	  (argc << 32) | envc for audit consumers that want process image
	  transition telemetry without copying argument contents or affecting
	  exec admission.

config SECURITY_HARDENING_CHDIR_AUDIT
	bool "Record successful directory-change events"
	default y
	help
	  Record successful chdir and fchdir operations into the hardening
	  event ring after the current working directory is committed. The
	  destination inode is stored as the event object for audit consumers
	  that want filesystem movement telemetry without affecting directory
	  change admission.

config SECURITY_HARDENING_MOUNT_AUDIT
	bool "Record successful mount administration events"
	default y
	help
	  Record successful mount, remount, bind, move_mount, fsmount, and
	  mount_setattr operations into the hardening event ring after the VFS
	  operation succeeds. The target path inode is stored as the event
	  object for audit consumers that want mount administration telemetry
	  without changing mount admission policy.

config SECURITY_HARDENING_USER_WX
	bool "Deny writable executable userspace mappings"
	default y
	help
	  Deny mmap() and mprotect() transitions that create writable and
	  executable userspace mappings. Also deny adding execute permission to
	  a mapping that is currently writable.
	  Profiles can grant a narrow JIT exception for private anonymous
	  write-to-execute transitions without allowing simultaneous W+X.
	  This option provides the default no-ambient-WX baseline.

config SECURITY_HARDENING_MEMFD_NOEXEC
	bool "Force non-executable memfd defaults"
	default y
	depends on MEMFD_CREATE && SYSCTL
	help
	  Force vm.memfd_noexec to the enforced no-exec mode. Unflagged
	  memfd_create() calls receive MFD_NOEXEC_SEAL semantics, explicit
	  MFD_EXEC requests are rejected, and the sysctl cannot be lowered by a
	  descendant pid namespace. This removes an ambient file-backed
	  executable-memory source while leaving explicit HDN JIT compatibility
	  policy in charge of legitimate executable memory transitions.

config SECURITY_HARDENING_MMAP_MIN_ADDR
	bool "Lock low-address mmap floor"
	default y
	depends on SYSCTL
	help
	  Keep vm.mmap_min_addr at or above 64 KiB so a later privileged sysctl
	  write cannot remove the low-address mapping barrier used to make NULL
	  pointer dereference exploitation harder. Higher product or LSM floors
	  remain supported.

config SECURITY_HARDENING_RWXMAP_AUDIT
	bool "Record denied writable-executable mapping attempts"
	default y
	depends on SECURITY_HARDENING_USER_WX
	help
	  Emit a dedicated hardening event when the W+X memory policy denies an
	  explicit mmap() or mprotect() request that combines write and execute
	  permission. The ordinary USER_WX_MAPPING denial event is still
	  emitted for policy and response handling; this option gives desktop
	  and log consumers a stable grsec-class RWX-map audit signal.

config SECURITY_HARDENING_UNPRIV_BPF
	bool "Require privilege for BPF program load"
	default y
	select BPF_JIT if BPF_SYSCALL && (HAVE_CBPF_JIT || HAVE_EBPF_JIT)
	select BPF_JIT_ALWAYS_ON if BPF_SYSCALL && HAVE_EBPF_JIT
	help
	  Require BPF program loads to come from a privileged kernel caller or
	  from a task that already passed the BPF capability/token checks.
	  This keeps BPF program loading behind a central authority gate.
	  Existing BPF verifier and LSM checks still run after this gate.
	  On architectures with eBPF JIT support, permanently enable the JIT,
	  remove the interpreter, force constant blinding, and hide JIT
	  kallsyms so privileged BPF service support does not reintroduce a
	  downgradeable execution surface.

config SECURITY_HARDENING_UNPRIV_PERF_KERNEL
	bool "Require privilege for kernel perf visibility"
	default y
	help
	  Require CAP_PERFMON for perf_event_open() requests that can observe
	  kernel execution or namespace metadata.
	  User-only perf use remains governed by existing kernel checks.
	  The goal is a single hardening authority for kernel observability.

config SECURITY_HARDENING_PTRACE_READEXEC
	bool "Deny forced reads of execute-only mappings"
	default y
	help
	  Require explicit profile authority before ptrace-like forced remote
	  reads can read userspace mappings that are executable but not readable
	  in the target VMA. This preserves ordinary ptrace admission as a
	  separate policy decision while preventing ptrace and active
	  /proc/pid/mem reads from bypassing execute-only userspace mappings by
	  default.

config SECURITY_HARDENING_PTRACE_AUDIT
	bool "Audit ptrace attach attempts"
	default y
	help
	  Emit structured hardening events for ptrace attach and seize attempts
	  after the hardening profile gate has allowed the request. Successful
	  attaches are recorded as allow-audit events; later upstream denials
	  are recorded as deny-audit events with the kernel error preserved.

config SECURITY_HARDENING_UNPRIV_USERNS
	bool "Gate user namespace creation by hardening profile"
	default y
	help
	  Require explicit profile authority for user namespace creation.
	  This keeps unprivileged namespace creation behind the same central
	  attack-surface policy as BPF, perf, ptrace, and debug interfaces.
	  Existing namespace limits, capability checks, sysctls, and LSM
	  checks still apply afterward.

config SECURITY_HARDENING_NAMESPACE_PROFILES
	bool "Enforce namespace profile inheritance"
	default y
	help
	  Attach hardening profile IDs to user namespaces, inherit the current
	  profile when creating a user namespace, and deny namespace creation or
	  namespace joins that would widen the caller's profile unless the
	  profile has namespace administration authority. This also covers
	  mount namespace files created through open_tree(OPEN_TREE_NAMESPACE).

config SECURITY_HARDENING_MODULE_FILE_ONLY
	bool "Deny raw init_module images"
	default y
	help
	  Deny init_module() loading from an anonymous userspace memory image.
	  finit_module() remains the intended path so module admission can be
	  tied to file identity, signatures, and package provenance.
	  This establishes file-backed module admission as the default contract.
	  When an active hardening policy is loaded, file-backed module loads
	  must also match the policy's exact module trust table.

config SECURITY_HARDENING_MODULE_AUTOLOAD
	bool "Gate module autoload requests by hardening profile"
	default y
	depends on MODULES
	help
	  Require MODULE_AUTOLOAD profile authority before request_module()
	  can ask the usermode helper to satisfy a kernel module alias. This
	  keeps implicit module expansion behind policy while leaving explicit
	  file-backed module loading under the separate MODULE_LOAD authority.
	  Writes to the kernel.modprobe sysctl also require policy-admin context
	  and MODULE_AUTOLOAD authority, so a restricted root profile cannot
	  redirect future helper execution. Existing LSM, module signature, and
	  modprobe behavior still run after this gate for authorized autoload
	  requests. Authorized helpers receive bounded HDN_MODULE_* environment
	  context describing the requested alias, requester ids, and HDN profile.

config SECURITY_HARDENING_MODULE_AUTOLOAD_PATH
	bool "Validate module autoload helper paths"
	default y
	depends on SECURITY_HARDENING_MODULE_AUTOLOAD
	help
	  Require writes to kernel.modprobe to either disable autoload with an
	  empty path or select an absolute, symlink-free, root-owned helper
	  through non-group/world-writable ancestry. This keeps an authorized
	  policy-admin operation from accidentally turning future module
	  autoloads into execution through a mutable path.

config SECURITY_HARDENING_PATH_DEFAULTS
	bool "Use hardened path-handling defaults"
	default y
	help
	  Default and lock the existing protected symlink, hardlink, FIFO, and
	  regular file sysctls to hardened values. These upstream checks block
	  common sticky-directory link and replacement attacks without requiring
	  userspace policy prompts.

config SECURITY_HARDENING_SYMLINK_OWNER
	bool "Deny symlink target-owner mismatches"
	default y
	help
	  Track foreign, non-root symlink owners during pathname resolution and
	  deny a completed lookup when any tracked symlink owner differs from the
	  final target owner. A caller may follow its own symlinks subject to the
	  target's normal permissions. This complements the upstream
	  protected_symlinks sticky-directory check while preserving generated
	  per-user links used by desktop sessions and service managers.

config SECURITY_HARDENING_TRUSTED_EXEC
	bool "Support trusted-path execution profiles"
	default y
	help
	  Allow profiles to require executable files and their dentry and
	  mountpoint ancestor directories to be root-owned and not group/world
	  writable. Profiles can grant a bypass authority for package managers,
	  build systems, and other trusted tooling that intentionally executes
	  from writable locations.

config SECURITY_HARDENING_CHROOT_CONTAINMENT
	bool "Harden chroot containment"
	default y
	help
	  Allow profiles to harden chroot use by denying repeated chroot,
	  fchdir/chdir escapes outside the current root, and pivot_root from a
	  chroot unless the profile has explicit chroot administration
	  authority. Contained chroots also deny chmod, device-node mknod,
	  shared-memory attach, priority increases, and bad directory renames
	  by default, and block abstract AF_UNIX socket connections to sockets
	  bound outside the current root. They also reduce dangerous
	  capabilities and hide outside-root tasks from PID lookup/procfs views
	  while denying explicit signal/file-owner targeting outside the current
	  root. The reduced capability set covers module loading, raw I/O,
	  broad system administration, reboot/time changes, raw networking, tty
	  configuration, IPC ownership override, file capability writes, kernel
	  log control, MAC administration, immutable-file changes, process
	  accounting, and network administration. Successful chroot calls place
	  cwd at the new root.

config SECURITY_HARDENING_TIME_ADMIN
	bool "Gate system time changes by hardening profile"
	default y
	help
	  Require TIME_ADMIN profile authority after ordinary CAP_SYS_TIME and
	  LSM checks but before userspace can set wall-clock time, change the
	  kernel timezone, or discipline system time through adjtimex. This
	  keeps log, certificate, and freshness-sensitive time mutation behind a
	  narrow service authority while leaving clock reads and read-only time
	  queries untouched.

config SECURITY_HARDENING_TIME_AUDIT
	bool "Record successful system time changes"
	default y
	help
	  Record successful wall-clock, timezone, and mutable adjtimex changes
	  into the hardening event ring after ordinary kernel and LSM checks
	  succeed. The time-operation code is stored as the event object for
	  audit consumers that want time mutation telemetry without changing
	  admission policy.

config SECURITY_HARDENING_RESOURCE_ADMIN
	bool "Gate privileged resource limit raises by hardening profile"
	default y
	help
	  Require RESOURCE_ADMIN profile authority after ordinary
	  CAP_SYS_RESOURCE and LSM checks before a task can raise a hard
	  resource limit through setrlimit or prlimit. Soft-limit changes and
	  limit lowering remain governed by normal kernel behavior so ordinary
	  applications can tune their own process limits without a policy
	  prompt.

config SECURITY_HARDENING_RESOURCE_AUDIT
	bool "Record successful resource-limit changes"
	default y
	help
	  Record successful setrlimit and prlimit writes into the hardening event
	  ring after ordinary kernel and LSM checks succeed. The resource number
	  is stored as the event object for audit consumers that want process
	  resource telemetry without changing admission policy.

config SECURITY_HARDENING_BLOCK_DEVICE_RESTRICT
	bool "Gate raw block-device access by hardening profile"
	default y
	help
	  Require BLOCK_DEVICE_ACCESS profile authority before userspace can
	  open a block special file directly. Mounted filesystems and other
	  in-kernel block-device users keep using the ordinary block layer
	  helpers, while raw device reads and writes remain behind explicit
	  service authority. Privileged block-device administration commands,
	  including partition table updates, discard/zeroout operations, block
	  tracing control, block crypto key control, persistent reservation
	  writes, driver-specific fallback ioctls, and block io_uring discard
	  additionally require BLOCK_DEVICE_ADMIN so products can allow
	  inspection without allowing mutation.

config SECURITY_HARDENING_FORK_AUDIT
	bool "Record fork and clone events"
	default n
	help
	  Record successful fork, vfork, and clone operations into the
	  hardening event ring without changing fork admission. The child pid
	  is stored as the event object for audit consumers that explicitly want
	  complete process-creation telemetry. This is opt-in because a normal
	  desktop creates enough processes to crowd out higher-value security
	  events even when ring and journal flood control are active.

config SECURITY_HARDENING_FORKFAIL_AUDIT
	bool "Record failed fork attempts"
	default y
	help
	  Record failed fork, vfork, and clone attempts into the hardening
	  event ring when process creation is rejected because of process
	  count or memory pressure. The errno field stores the specific
	  rejection reason for audit consumers.

config SECURITY_HARDENING_FS_PROTECTION_AUDIT
	bool "Record protected link and sticky-file denials"
	default y
	help
	  Record denials from upstream protected symlink, protected hardlink,
	  protected FIFO, and protected regular-file sticky-directory checks
	  into the hardening event ring. This does not change the upstream
	  access-control decision; it gives desktop and log consumers stable
	  HDN event names for those filesystem protections.

config SECURITY_HARDENING_SIGNAL_AUDIT
	bool "Record severe signal events"
	default y
	help
	  Record severe fault-style signals into the hardening event ring
	  without changing signal delivery. This covers SIGSEGV, SIGILL,
	  SIGABRT, and SIGBUS so desktop crash reporters and recovery tooling
	  can consume structured crash telemetry without scraping kernel logs.
	  The event object packs the signal number with the low bits of the
	  reported fault address.

config SECURITY_HARDENING_SETXID
	bool "Propagate multithreaded root credential drops"
	default y
	help
	  When one thread in a multithreaded process changes its real uid from
	  root to non-root, queue same-process credential adoption work for the
	  sibling threads. This keeps non-glibc or partially custom runtimes from
	  leaving older sibling threads running with root credentials after the
	  process believes it has dropped privilege.

config SECURITY_HARDENING_BRUTE
	bool "Throttle repeated privileged exec crashes"
	default y
	help
	  Record repeated crash signals from privileged exec transitions and
	  temporarily deny new executions of the same file by the same uid and
	  target profile after a short burst. Also mark same-binary daemon
	  parents after a worker crash so later forks pause before launching
	  more workers. This limits brute-force attempts against setuid,
	  file-capability, and forking daemon targets without adding prompts or
	  userspace policy decisions to the crash path.

config SECURITY_HARDENING_KERNEL_LOCKOUT
	bool "Lock out users after kernel-surface crash signals"
	default y
	help
	  When a kernel-surface fault is reported, ban the originating uid until
	  reboot. A banned uid's existing tasks are killed, and later fork,
	  exec, or credential-entry attempts for that uid are denied. This gives
	  the system a hard stop against repeated kernel exploitation attempts
	  without exposing a user-facing prompt or requiring desktop policy
	  decisions in the fault path.

	  Root-origin and interrupt-context faults cannot be attributed to a
	  bannable uid; whether those escalate to a full panic is controlled by
	  SECURITY_HARDENING_KERNEL_LOCKOUT_PANIC.

config SECURITY_HARDENING_KERNEL_LOCKOUT_PANIC
	bool "Panic on unattributable kernel-surface faults"
	depends on SECURITY_HARDENING_KERNEL_LOCKOUT
	default n
	help
	  Escalate a kernel-surface fault to a full kernel panic when it
	  originates from a root-owned task or from interrupt context, where no
	  unprivileged uid can be banned. This is the strongest anti-exploitation
	  posture but turns any latent in-kernel oops from a root process into a
	  machine-wide denial of service, so it is unsuitable as an interactive
	  daily-driver default. Product/server images that want oops=panic-style
	  behavior can enable it (equivalently, set panic_on_oops); the
	  interactive baseline leaves it off so a single survivable oops does not
	  take the system down, while the uid-ban protection above still applies
	  to unprivileged faults.

config SECURITY_HARDENING_SUID_SPECTRE
	bool "Force speculation controls for privileged exec"
	default y
	depends on X86
	help
	  Force user-controllable Spectre V2 and Speculative Store Bypass
	  mitigations when a non-root caller executes a setuid, setgid, or
	  file-capability helper. This gives privileged helper processes a
	  secure default without requiring users or package maintainers to
	  understand per-process prctl speculation controls.

config SECURITY_HARDENING_SYSVIPC
	bool "Harden overly permissive System V IPC objects"
	default y
	depends on SYSVIPC
	help
	  Deny cross-uid or cross-gid access to System V IPC objects whose
	  mode bits grant broad world or group access beyond the creator or
	  current owner. Profiles can grant IPC_ADMIN as a narrow bypass for
	  compatibility services. Existing IPC permissions, capabilities, and
	  LSM checks still run as usual.

config SECURITY_HARDENING_POSIX_MQUEUE
	bool "Harden overly permissive POSIX message queues"
	default y
	depends on POSIX_MQUEUE
	help
	  Deny cross-uid or cross-gid access to POSIX message queue files
	  whose mode bits grant broad world or group access beyond the creator
	  or current owner. Profiles can grant IPC_ADMIN as a narrow bypass for
	  compatibility services. Existing VFS permissions, capabilities, and
	  LSM checks still run as usual.

config SECURITY_HARDENING_TTY_INJECTION
	bool "Gate terminal input injection ioctls"
	default y
	depends on TTY
	help
	  Require TTY_INJECT profile authority for terminal input injection
	  ioctls such as TIOCSTI and VT TIOCLINUX. This removes ambient
	  same-session command injection while still allowing trusted desktop
	  brokers or accessibility services to receive an explicit authority.
	  The legacy TIOCSTI and line-discipline autoload sysctls are forced
	  off and locked while this hardening is enabled.

config SECURITY_HARDENING_TTY_ACCESS
	bool "Gate non-controlling terminal descriptor access"
	default y
	depends on TTY
	help
	  Require TTY_ACCESS profile authority before a task reads, writes, or
	  ioctls a terminal that is not its controlling terminal. PTY masters
	  remain available for terminal emulators. PTY allocator and explicit
	  descriptor-transfer provenance identifies the exact setup broker; a
	  session's direct parent then retains cleanup access after the controlling
	  session exits. Ordinary fork inheritance does not grant broker access,
	  while controlling-terminal access remains available for shells and
	  console applications. This blocks inherited or preopened descriptor abuse
	  without asking normal desktop users to manage terminal policy.

config SECURITY_HARDENING_VM86
	bool "Gate x86 VM86 mode"
	default y
	depends on VM86
	help
	  Require RAW_IO profile authority before 32-bit x86 tasks can enter
	  Virtual 8086 mode or use the VM86 IRQ helper commands. Existing
	  mmap_min_addr, capability, lockdown, and LSM checks still run as
	  usual; this adds the same profile contract used for port I/O and raw
	  CPU device access.

config SECURITY_HARDENING_FUSE_RESTRICT
	bool "Gate FUSE filesystem mounts"
	default y
	help
	  Require FUSE_MOUNT profile authority before userspace can create a
	  FUSE or FUSE block-device mount. This keeps user-backed filesystem
	  servers behind a narrow authority instead of treating them as ordinary
	  mount administration.

config SECURITY_HARDENING_ROFS
	bool "Prevent read-only mount relaxation without authority"
	default y
	help
	  Require ROFS_RELAX profile authority before a read-only mount or
	  read-only superblock can be made writable again. This supports product
	  policies that make system mounts read-only after boot while preserving
	  a narrow compatibility bypass for trusted update tooling.

config SECURITY_HARDENING_USB_RESTRICT
	bool "Gate new USB device attachment and monitoring"
	default y
	depends on USB_SUPPORT
	help
	  Require USB_ATTACH profile authority before newly connected USB
	  devices are enumerated, and USB_MONITOR profile authority before a
	  task can open usbmon capture interfaces. Root hubs and devices
	  already discovered before policy commit remain governed by the normal
	  USB core state; runtime insertions are blocked early in the hub
	  attach path unless a trusted profile, broker, or boot policy grants
	  the authority.

config SECURITY_HARDENING_SOCKET_RESTRICT
	bool "Gate non-local socket operations by profile"
	default y
	depends on NET
	help
	  Require explicit profile authorities for non-local socket creation,
	  outbound connection attempts, and server-side bind/listen/accept
	  operations. AF_UNIX/AF_LOCAL sockets remain available by default so
	  normal desktop IPC does not depend on network authorities. Existing
	  protocol, capability, cgroup, and LSM checks still run after this
	  hardening gate.

config SECURITY_HARDENING_NET_BLACKHOLE
	bool "Blackhole remote closed-port TCP/UDP responses"
	default y
	depends on NET
	help
	  Suppress scanner-visible TCP resets and ICMP destination-unreachable
	  replies for remote packets that target closed TCP/UDP endpoints or
	  unsupported IPv4 protocols. Loopback traffic remains exempt so local
	  diagnostics, desktop IPC, and developer tools keep normal closed-port
	  behavior. This also shortens LAST_ACK retry behavior to limit stale
	  sockets caused by blackholed remote resets.

config SECURITY_HARDENING_TCP_NO_SIMULT_CONNECT
	bool "Disable TCP simultaneous connect"
	default y
	depends on NET
	help
	  Disable the crossed-SYN TCP simultaneous-open path. Normal client and
	  server TCP handshakes are unaffected; the uncommon client-client
	  simultaneous connect behavior is removed as an invisible hardening
	  default.

config SECURITY_HARDENING_PROC_HIDE
	bool "Hide other users' procfs task directories by profile"
	default y
	depends on PROC_FS
	help
	  Hide /proc task directories for processes owned by other users unless
	  the current profile has PROC_DISCLOSE authority. This gives the distro
	  a secure default similar to hidepid behavior without requiring every
	  product image to remember a special procfs mount option.

config SECURITY_HARDENING_PROC_TASK_GID
	int "Global proc task-view group id"
	default -1
	range -1 2147483647
	depends on SECURITY_HARDENING_PROC_HIDE
	help
	  Optionally allow members of one global group to see other users'
	  procfs task directories and mint pidfds without granting broader
	  PROC_DISCLOSE authority or per-profile PROC_TASK_VIEW authority.
	  Set this to -1 to disable the global group. Product images can
	  override the configured value at boot with hdn.proc_task_gid=GID.

config SECURITY_HARDENING_PROC_TASK_MODES
	bool "Restrict proc task directory modes to the task-view group"
	default y
	depends on SECURITY_HARDENING_PROC_HIDE
	help
	  When a global proc task-view group is configured, expose procfs task
	  directories as owner-and-task-view-group traversable instead of
	  world-traversable. This keeps proc entry metadata consistent with the
	  task visibility policy while leaving the feature inert until
	  SECURITY_HARDENING_PROC_TASK_GID or hdn.proc_task_gid selects a group.

config SECURITY_HARDENING_PROC_MEMMAP
	bool "Redact procfs address maps without disclosure authority"
	default y
	depends on PROC_FS
	help
	  Redact ASLR-relevant addresses and offsets from /proc/<pid>/maps,
	  /proc/<pid>/smaps, /proc/<pid>/smaps_rollup, /proc/<pid>/numa_maps,
	  /proc/<pid>/stat, and /proc/<pid>/pagemap when a process reads a
	  different address space without PROC_DISCLOSE authority, and deny
	  address-query ioctls for that same case. System resource maps in
	  /proc/ioports and /proc/iomem also keep their device/resource names
	  while collapsing ranges to zero without disclosure authority. Global
	  page-monitoring metadata in /proc/kpagecount, /proc/kpageflags, and
	  /proc/kpagecgroup is denied without disclosure authority. Self reads
	  are left intact for compatibility, while desktop tools keep useful
	  mapping names and permissions without learning target addresses.
	  Privileged execs are also limited to a 512 KiB copied argument/
	  environment stack and an 8 MiB stack resource limit so a setuid,
	  setgid, or file-capability helper cannot consume the stack entropy that
	  these procfs redactions are meant to protect.

config SECURITY_HARDENING_SYMBOL_LOOKUP
	bool "Gate and isolate kernel symbol lookup"
	default y
	depends on KALLSYMS
	help
	  Require PROC_DISCLOSE authority before tracing, probe, and BPF
	  interfaces expose or resolve kernel symbol names for userspace. Raw
	  kallsyms declarations live in a separate internal header, while the
	  ordinary header exposes only policy-redacting formatters. This keeps
	  tracefs/debugfs or BPF access from implicitly granting a kallsyms
	  oracle and makes every in-tree raw lookup dependency explicit.

config SECURITY_HARDENING_PROC_IPADDR
	bool "Expose accepted TCP peer addresses in procfs"
	default y
	depends on PROC_FS && INET
	help
	  Add an owner-readable /proc/<pid>/ipaddr file that reports the
	  IPv4 peer address most recently accepted by the process. The address
	  is handed to one forked child and then cleared from the acceptor so
	  per-connection worker processes can be attributed without exposing a
	  terminal-facing feature to normal desktop users.

config SECURITY_HARDENING_RAND_THREADSTACK
	bool "Randomize anonymous thread-stack placement gaps"
	default y
	help
	  Insert a small random unmapped gap before anonymous non-fixed
	  MAP_STACK and stack-like MAP_GROWSDOWN allocations when process
	  address-space randomization is enabled. This gives pthread-style
	  and legacy grow-down stacks kernel-chosen placement entropy without
	  requiring applications, package scripts, or users to understand
	  another runtime control.

config SECURITY_HARDENING_USERFAULTFD_RESTRICT
	bool "Lock unprivileged userfaultfd kernel-fault handling off"
	default y
	depends on USERFAULTFD && SYSCTL
	help
	  Keep vm.unprivileged_userfaultfd pinned at 0 so unprivileged
	  userfaultfd syscall users must request UFFD_USER_MODE_ONLY and cannot
	  register handlers for faults taken from kernel mode. Access through
	  /dev/userfaultfd remains controlled by normal device permissions for
	  trusted virtualization and checkpoint/restore services.

config SECURITY_HARDENING_IO_URING_SYSCTL_LOCK
	bool "Make io_uring disable sysctl monotonic"
	default y
	depends on IO_URING && SYSCTL
	help
	  Allow product policy, image sealing, or recovery flows to tighten
	  kernel.io_uring_disabled while preventing later sysctl writes from
	  lowering the restriction again. This keeps ordinary io_uring usable by
	  default while making emergency or image-specific io_uring lockdown a
	  one-way operation until reboot.

config SECURITY_HARDENING_EXPLOIT_MITIGATIONS
	bool "Enable exploit mitigation baseline"
	default y
	select SECURITY_YAMA
	select VMAP_STACK if HAVE_ARCH_VMAP_STACK
	select RANDOMIZE_KSTACK_OFFSET_DEFAULT if HAVE_ARCH_RANDOMIZE_KSTACK_OFFSET
	select STACKPROTECTOR_STRONG if HAVE_STACKPROTECTOR
	select STRICT_KERNEL_RWX if ARCH_HAS_STRICT_KERNEL_RWX
	select STRICT_MODULE_RWX if ARCH_HAS_STRICT_MODULE_RWX && MODULES
	select LIST_HARDENED
	select PAGE_TABLE_CHECK if ARCH_SUPPORTS_PAGE_TABLE_CHECK && EXCLUSIVE_SYSTEM_RAM
	select PAGE_TABLE_CHECK_ENFORCED if ARCH_SUPPORTS_PAGE_TABLE_CHECK && EXCLUSIVE_SYSTEM_RAM
	help
	  Enable the upstream exploit-mitigation baseline expected by this
	  hardening contract layer. This makes the distro profile prefer
	  memory initialization, hardened usercopy, fortified string/memory
	  helpers, slab freelist hardening and randomization, non-merged slab
	  caches, enforced page-table checking on supported architectures,
	  vmapped kernel stacks, kernel stack offset randomization, strong
	  stack protector, structure layout randomization, strict kernel and
	  module W^X, locked Yama relational ptrace scope, locked dmesg
	  restriction, locked setuid coredump suppression, kernel
	  pointer/symbol disclosure restriction, a locked low-address mmap floor,
	  locked unprivileged userfaultfd kernel-fault handling, monotonic
	  io_uring disablement, and kCFI when the selected architecture and
	  compiler support them.

	  This is intentionally a build-time default layer rather than a policy
	  prompt. Product policy can decide who may perform dangerous actions;
	  this option makes corruption and information disclosure harder before
	  policy is involved.

config SECURITY_HARDENING_AUTOSLAB_TEST
	bool "HDN typed allocation domain fixtures (test kernels only)"
	depends on SECURITY_HARDENING_AUTOSLAB && KUNIT=y
	default n
	help
	  Exercise isolation and reallocation through the real allocator.
	  This does not enable fault injection in production kernels.

config SECURITY_HARDENING_AUTOSLAB_MODULE_TEST
	tristate "HDN module allocation-domain fixture (test kernels only)"
	depends on SECURITY_HARDENING_AUTOSLAB_TEST && MODULES && m
	default n
	help
	  Build a disposable-guest module whose compiler-typed allocations and
	  attempted indirect request for a built-in domain must both resolve to
	  the permanent lower-trust module domain. The loader also uses this
	  module as the positive input for metadata-section rejection mutations.

config SECURITY_HARDENING_SLAB_JITTER_TEST
	bool "HDN critical-object corruption fixtures (test kernels only)"
	depends on SECURITY_HARDENING_SLAB_JITTER && DEBUG_KERNEL && MODULES
	default n
	help
	  Build a loadable driver for deterministic placement and destructive
	  corruption probes. Load only in a disposable guest. The seeded class
	  is separate from all production object classes.

endif

config SECURITY_HARDENING_EFI_TEST
	tristate "Read-only EFI private-stack runtime probes"
	depends on SECURITY_HARDENING_PRIVATE_STACKS && EFI && DEBUG_KERNEL && m
	default n
	help
	  A signed post-boot module for disposable EFI test guests. Compare
	  stack-backed and heap-backed SecureBoot reads, required-size queries,
	  and GetTime output through the actual EFI runtime interfaces. Does
	  not write variables, reset the machine, or install firmware handlers.
